19:T89b,## Two ways to share a shape

Both let you program against a type without knowing the concrete class, but they answer different questions.

## Abstract class, a half-built base

An **abstract class** cannot be instantiated (`new Game()` won't compile). It is a *partial* class meant to be **extended**. It can carry:

- **fields / state**, e.g. `# players`, shared by every subclass.
- **constructors**, to initialise that state.
- **both** concrete methods (real code subclasses inherit) **and** abstract methods (signatures subclasses must fill in).

```
abstract class Game {
    protected int players;
    public final void play() {      // concrete: shared template
        initialize();
        loop();
        endGame();
    }
    protected abstract void initialize();   // subclass fills in
    protected abstract void endGame();
}
```

Reach for it when subtypes are genuinely an **"is-a"** of one another and you want to **hand them shared code + state**.

## Interface, a pure contract

An **interface** is a **"can-do"** capability: a list of method signatures with **no instance state**. Every method is abstract (modern languages allow `default` bodies, but still no fields). A class declares it **implements** the interface and supplies the bodies.

The killer feature: a class can implement **many** interfaces, so the same type can gain **unrelated** capabilities, `Savable`, `Rankable`, `Printable`without forcing them into one family tree.

## The rule that decides it

- You can **extend exactly ONE** abstract class (single inheritance).
- You can **implement MANY** interfaces.

## Comparison

- **Instantiate**, abstract: no · interface: no
- **Instance state / fields**, abstract: yes · interface: no
- **Constructor**, abstract: yes · interface: no
- **Concrete method bodies**, abstract: yes · interface: defaults only
- **How many per class**, abstract: one · interface: many
- **Models**, abstract: *is-a* (identity) · interface: *can-do* (capability)

> Decision guide: need **shared code/state plus one identity**? → **abstract class**. Need a **capability mixable across unrelated classes**? → **interface**. When in doubt, prefer the interface, it couples less.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-foundations","activeLesson":"abstract-vs-interface"}],["$","$L18","abstract-vs-interface",{"lesson":{"id":"abstract-vs-interface","patternId":"lld-foundations","title":"Abstract class vs Interface","kind":"concept","difficulty":"Core","statement":"An abstract class is a half-built base you EXTEND (one parent, shared code + state); an interface is a pure contract you IMPLEMENT (many of them, no state). Knowing which to reach for is a classic interview question.","companies":["Amazon","Microsoft","Oracle","Adobe"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"Extend one, implement many","code":["abstract class Game {","    protected int players;","    public final void play() { initialize(); endGame(); }","    protected abstract void initialize();","    protected abstract void endGame();","}","interface Savable  { void save(); }","interface Rankable { int  rank(); }","","class Chess extends Game implements Savable, Rankable { ... }"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Game is an ABSTRACT class: it holds state (# players), a CONCRETE template method play(), and ABSTRACT methods (marked *) that subclasses must fill in. You can never `new` it directly.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"game","name":"Game","stereotype":"abstract","fields":["# players: int"],"methods":["+ play()","# initialize()*","# endGame()*"],"x":0.9,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"savable","name":"Savable","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ save()"],"x":2.4,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent2"},{"id":"chess","name":"Chess","methods":["# initialize()","# endGame()","+ save()"],"x":1.4,"y":1.7},{"id":"comparable","name":"Rankable","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ rank()"],"x":2.4,"y":1.55,"tint":"accent2"}],"relations":[{"id":"ext","from":"chess","to":"game","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"imp1","from":"chess","to":"savable","kind":"implement"},{"id":"imp2","from":"chess","to":"comparable","kind":"implement"}],"shownClassIds":["game"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["game"]},"vars":[["abstract","cannot be new-ed"],["state +","concrete + abstract"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":7,"narration":"Savable is an INTERFACE: a pure contract, just save(), no fields, no state, no bodies. It says what a class can DO, never how or with what data.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["game","savable"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["savable"]},"vars":[["interface","pure contract"],["state","none"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":10,"narration":"Chess EXTENDS Game, a solid hollow triangle. It inherits play() and the players state for free, and supplies the abstract steps initialize()/endGame(). You extend exactly ONE abstract class.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["game","savable","chess"],"shownRelIds":["ext"],"activeIds":["chess"]},"vars":[["extends","1 class"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":10,"narration":"Chess also IMPLEMENTS Savable AND Rankable, dashed triangles. A single class can implement MANY interfaces, gaining unrelated capabilities (save, rank) without changing its parentage.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["game","savable","chess","comparable"],"shownRelIds":["ext","imp1","imp2"],"activeIds":["chess","savable","comparable"]},"vars":[["implements","many interfaces"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"The rule: need shared code/state and one identity → reach for an ABSTRACT class. Need a capability you can mix across unrelated classes → reach for an INTERFACE.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["game","savable","chess","comparable"],"shownRelIds":["ext","imp1","imp2"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["is-a + code","abstract class"],["can-do","interface"]]}]}]}}]]}]
